From: FlipperEmu
Subject: [PATCH] eeprom_at24c: keep the address counter within the memory

An EEPROM of more than 256 bytes takes two address bytes. Each one is
shifted into the address counter as it comes, and the counter is
reduced to the EEPROM's size when the second has come. A write that
ends after the first byte (a STOP, or a new START) leaves the counter
as it was shifted, the old low byte as the new high byte, with the
count of address bytes back at zero.

The next read takes mem[cur] with that counter. For an EEPROM of 4096
bytes that is up to 60 KiB past the end of its memory: a byte of QEMU's
heap goes to the guest. It is one byte per round, since the read
reduces the counter then, and the guest chooses the place with the
address that it sets before. (A write does not leave the memory this
way: it begins with address bytes, and a whole address is reduced.)

Reduce the counter when a START or a STOP ends an address that is not
whole.

Seen with the Flipper Zero machine and an EEPROM of 4096 bytes on the
GPIO header's I2C bus, whose backing file was all 0xA5: sixteen reads,
each after an address of one byte, gave 0x00. With this patch they give
0xA5.

Signed-off-by: FlipperEmu
---
--- a/hw/nvram/eeprom_at24c.c
+++ b/hw/nvram/eeprom_at24c.c
@@ -66,6 +66,13 @@
     switch (event) {
     case I2C_START_SEND:
     case I2C_FINISH:
+        /*
+         * An address that was cut short (one byte of two) is in the
+         * counter all the same: keep the counter within the memory.
+         */
+        if (ee->haveaddr > 0 && ee->haveaddr < ee->asize) {
+            ee->cur %= ee->rsize;
+        }
         ee->haveaddr = 0;
         /* fallthrough */
     case I2C_START_RECV:
